1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. a3 $6 {An unusual move.} Bc5 5. Bb5 O-O ({
Better looks} 5... d6) 6. d3 h6 {Defensive play, but I worried about the pin
of the black knight} 7. Bxc6 dxc6 8. O-O Bg4 9. h3 Bh5 ({Now, here should
happen} 9... Bxf3 10. Qxf3 {but the black knight on the kingside has no good
squares}) 10. Be3 ({Here the pawn advance} 10. g4 Bg6 {looks more logical as
the black light squared bishop is entombed and the white kingside pawns are
nevertheless weak.}) 10... Qe7 11. Qe2 Rad8 12. Bxc5 Qxc5 13. Qe3 {White is
looking for exchange and a better endgame.} Qe7 {The retreat is without a real
alternative. Now White gets a moderate advantage.} 14. Nh2 Bg6 15. Qxa7 b6 $5 {
I hope for some counterplay for the marginal pawn, so the white queen has some
uncomfortable suit.} 16. Qa4 Qe6 {Defending the c-pawn and improving the
position of the black queen.} 17. Rfd1 Rd4 18. b4 Rfd8 19. Nf3 R4d7 20. Nd2 Nh5
{White has done some floppy moves, sensible, but without force. His pieces
stand outside and passive. Now it's time for black to start an kingside attack,
still controlling the centre.} 21. Ne2 Nf4 22. Nxf4 exf4 23. f3 $2 {A poor
move that weakens the dark squares in White's position. Although White has
still a knight and Black keeps only the dark-squared bishop, the black queen
can move around and tries to create some disorder.} Qf6 24. Qb3 Bh5 25. Rab1
Qd4+ {The black pieces could get aggressive stances. Now White has to stand
the pressure} 26. Kh2 Qf2 27. Qc3 Rd6 28. Rf1 Qg3+ 29. Kh1 {Now we see the
effect of the weak dark squares of White. The black plan is to put the rooks
on the kingside and to sacrify the useless bishop.} Rg6 30. Rg1 Rdd6 $6 {
Provokes the following move, but I don't appreciate the alternatives as well.}
31. e5 Rde6 32. Ne4 Qh4 33. d4 {It looks if White can ease his position.} Rxg2
$4 {A speculative strike in time trouble, that is a fatal error indeed. White
takes unfortunately with the wrong piece.} ({Not a possible solutiona is to
give the quality on g5 because the white knight is indeed very strong:} 33...
Rg5 34. Nxg5 hxg5 {but after} 35. Qe1 $1 {White has a technical winning
position}) ({Black should have played} 33... f5 {to keep the attack alive and
giving the poor rook for the strong knight} 34. exf6 Rxe4 35. fxe4 Rg3 $1 {
This leads to a terrible complicated play} 36. Qc4+ Bf7 37. Qf1 f3 {S1} 38. Kh2
Be6 $1 39. Rb3 Qf4 {with many threats for Black and probably a winning
position but none of the players had enough time to figure this out}) 34. Rxg2
$2 ({After} 34. Kxg2 $1 Rg6+ 35. Kh2 $1 {White is out of trouble and Black a
whole rook down.} ({Not} 35. Kf1 $2 Qxh3+ 36. Ke2 Qh2+ 37. Kd3 {as this looks
good for Black...})) 34... Qxh3+ 35. Kg1 Bxf3 36. Qd2 $4 ({Gives Black the win.
Necessary was} 36. Nf6+ Rxf6 37. Qxf3 Qxf3 38. exf6 {with a strong position
for Black but White can still fight.}) 36... Bxe4 37. Rf1 Bxg2 38. Qxg2 Rg6 39.
Rf2 Rxg2+ 40. Rxg2 Qe3+ 41. Kh2 g5 0-1

